  "text": "The biggest forest‑management challenge in this coastal province is getting new trees to grow on lands that have been logged, both on the main land and the nearby island. The area’s forests, dominated by a fast‑growing pine‑type tree together with a couple of other native conifers, supply almost half of the country’s annual timber and sit on very fertile soils. Because these cut‑over sites are easy to reach and can produce valuable wood quickly, they should be the first places to receive intensive care. Historically, the climate and soil, together with the biology of the native species, allowed seedlings to appear and grow rapidly after logging. Small clearings left by early horse‑drawn logging kept many mature and seed‑bearing trees standing, and the limited debris did not dry out, so natural regrowth was strong. With the shift to mechanized harvesters, a larger share of trees was removed and the remaining understory was heavily disturbed, greatly reducing the natural seed source and creating a serious regeneration problem."
